# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
spinoza-god.md:3: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: spinoza-god.logic.r3 and spinoza-god.logic.r3.2 have "◇¬god_exists → ¬essence_involves_existence"; spinoza-god.logic.r3.3 has "□(¬god_exists → ¬essence_involves_existence)"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
spinoza-god.md:3: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps spinoza-god.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "God's essence involves existence", "necessarily, (if it is not so that God, or substance consisting of infinite attributes, each expressing eternal and infinite essentiality, exists, then it is not so that God's essence involves existence)", "possibly, it is not so that God, or substance consisting of infinite attributes, each expressing eternal and infinite essentiality, exists" and "possibly, it is not so that God, or substance consisting of infinite attributes, each expressing eternal and infinite essentiality, exists" are false. Is S4, "Proof.--If this be denied, conceive, if possible, that God does not exist: then his essence does not involve existence.", true then?
spinoza-god.md:3: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: spinoza-god.logic.r3 and spinoza-god.logic.r3.2 have "essence_involves_existence"; spinoza-god.logic.r3.3 has "□essence_involves_existence"; they differ when only "God's essence involves existence" is true, which the first allows
spinoza-god.md:3: argue/modal-ambiguous: S5 is read spinoza-god.logic.r3 and spinoza-god.logic.r3.2 with no modality; spinoza-god.logic.r3.3 as what is necessary or possible: the text does not say which it means
spinoza-god.md:3: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps spinoza-god.logic.r3.3, to: Suppose "God's essence involves existence" is true and "necessarily, God's essence involves existence" is false. Is S5, "But this (Prop. vii.) is absurd.", true then?
